 means Tiger Fortress. The complex is also known as the Old Secretariat, from its later use as a government building.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Sher Garhi Palace","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Srinagar_Sher_Garhi_Palace_1890.png","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Srinagar_Sher_Garhi_Palace_1890.png","w":1200,"h":755,"author":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Srinagar_Sher_Garhi_Palace_1890.png"}},{"id":"srinagar_hari_parbat_fort","areaId":"srinagar","name":"Hari Parbat Fort","category":"monument","tags":["history","views"],"location":{"lat":34.10603,"lng":74.81628},"visitMinutes":90,"priceLevel":1,"photoSeed":"srinagar_hari_parbat_fort","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":70,"commonsNearby":11,"wikiPageviews":338,"wikidataSitelinks":4,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-21"},"hashtags":["hariparbatfort","srinagar"],"bestLight":"golden","summary":"Hari Parbat Fort is a historical fortification atop the Hari Parbat hill in Srinagar, Jammu and Kashmir, India. The fort was built during the late 18th/early 19th century by Atta Mohammad Khan, governor of Kashmir under Durrani rule.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Hari Parbat Fort","photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Hari_Parbat%2C_Srinagar%2C_J%26K.jpg/1280px-Hari_Parbat%2C_Srinagar%2C_J%26K.jpg","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Hari_Parbat%2C_Srinagar%2C_J%26K.jpg/500px-Hari_Parbat%2C_Srinagar%2C_J%26K.jpg","w":1200,"h":800,"author":"Mohamad Talib Bhat","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hari_Parbat,_Srinagar,_J%26K.jpg"}},{"id":"srinagar_zero_bridge","areaId":"srinagar","name":"Zero Bridge","category":"landmark","tags":["views","photo","sunset"],"location":{"lat":34.0698,"lng":74.83},"visitMinutes":30,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"srinagar_zero_bridge","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":1,"commonsNearby":210,"wikiPageviews":284,"wikidataSitelinks":3,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-21"},"hashtags":["zerobridge","srinagar"],"bestLight":"sunset","summary":"Zero Bridge is a wooden arch pedestrian bridge in Srinagar, Jammu and Kashmir, India. It spans the Jhelum River, connecting the Sonwar area on the north bank with Rajbagh on the south.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Zero Bridge","photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Zero_bridge.jpg/1280px-Zero_bridge.jpg","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Zero_bridge.jpg/500px-Zero_bridge.jpg","w":1200,"h":675,"author":"Sarkaaaar","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zero_bridge.jpg"}},{"id":"srinagar_nigeen_lake","areaId":"srinagar","name":"Nigeen Lake","category":"park","tags":["nature","photo"],"location":{"lat":34.11389,"lng":74.83222},"visitMinutes":60,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"srinagar_nigeen_lake","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":11,"commonsNearby":0,"wikiPageviews":392,"wikidataSitelinks":11,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-21"},"hashtags":["nigeenlake","srinagar"],"bestLight":"golden","summary":"Nigeen Lake is a lake located in Srinagar, Jammu and Kashmir, India. It is mildly eutrophic, a term that refers to a body of water rich in nutrients, which cause excessive growth of aquatic plants like algae, resulting in bacteria that consume nearly all the oxygen.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Nigeen Lake","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Nagin_Lake_%28Srinagar%29.jpg","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Nagin_Lake_%28Srinagar%29.jpg","w":1200,"h":900,"author":"Fjdelisle","license":"CC BY 3.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"h